# ---- The handshake: how this process reaches Stalwart ----
#
# These three are the only configuration a deployment passes in. They are the
# hand at the door: without them there is nothing to ask, so a boot that finds
# one missing stops and says which one, rather than guessing a host nobody runs.
#
# Base URL of your Stalwart server (scheme + host, no path). Gilbert discovers
# the JMAP session at <STALWART_URL>/.well-known/jmap.
STALWART_URL=https://mail.example.com

# The Master account (ADR 0003): a Stalwart account that acts inside mail and
# file storage for the groups it is in. It is also the installation's own
# account -- where the installation document below is kept -- and the server
# signs in as it at boot, so the password is the account's own, never an app
# password.
#
# Create the account and add it to a group in Stalwart's own administration:
# membership is the grant, and Gilbert never writes one.
#
#   npm run dev:mock:agent               # the demo stack, agent included
#   docker compose up                    # server and its agent, one container
#   docker compose --profile agent up    # the agent in its own container
#
# The password is also used to seal sessions onto impersonated accounts, so a
# deployment whose administrator is not this account needs nothing else for
# that: the pair is how every privileged write is made.
GILBERT_AGENT_ADDRESS=gilbert@example.com
GILBERT_AGENT_PASSWORD=

# Whether an upstream URL the server advertises is followed when it differs
# from STALWART_URL above. Off by default: only the path and query are taken
# from the advertised URL, and scheme, host and port come from STALWART_URL as
# written. A deployment that genuinely needs to reach Stalwart at a different
# origin than the one it was given sets this to 1.
# STALWART_FOLLOW_ADVERTISED_URLS=1

# ---- The container's own facts ----
#
# Where this process listens and what it may assume about its own filesystem.
# A boot reads these before anything else, and HOST and PORT *override* the
# installation document when the container states them: it is the process that
# knows which interface and port it was given. A container that states neither
# takes the document's `server.host` and `server.port`.

HOST=0.0.0.0
PORT=8080

# Assert that this instance is running as an immutable container: read-only
# root filesystem, nothing durable of its own, replaceable by its image. It is
# checked rather than taken on trust -- the probe writes to the filesystem to
# find out -- because the failure it guards against is silent: an instance that
# looks healthy and loses what it kept the moment it is replaced. Off by
# default. Running one looks like:
#
#   docker run --read-only --tmpfs /tmp -e IMMUTABLE=1 ...
#
# Nothing has to be kept here for a redeploy to lose nothing: every durable
# thing Gilbert holds is a document in Stalwart, sessions included.
# IMMUTABLE=1

# Whether this process starts the phone's Janus bridge beside itself. On by
# default (the bridge is part of this release, ADR 0023); a process that serves
# no phone -- the agent worker container -- sets it to 0. Installing it and the
# one port it needs: INSTALL.md.
# GILBERT_BRIDGE=0

# ---- The image's own facts ----
#
# What this process is running rather than what this installation decided:
# where the built web bundle sits, where *this* version's source can be had,
# and which Stalwart permission marks an administrator. The container sets the
# first itself; the other two take the defaults below unless a deployment states
# otherwise, and the source offer is the one a patched deployment must change.

# Where the built web bundle is. The container sets this itself; the variable is
# here for the deployment that puts it somewhere else.
# STATIC_DIR=/app/web/dist

# Where this instance's source can be had, shown to everyone who reaches it.
# Anyone deploying a patched Gilbert points this at their own tree, not at the
# one it was forked from.
# SOURCE_URL=https://github.com/sequico/gilbert

# The `/api/account` permission that marks a Stalwart admin (ADR 0001):
# `sysAccountCreate` appears in a principal's permission list exactly when the
# operator grants the admin role.
# GILBERT_ADMIN_PERMISSION=sysAccountCreate

# ---- The operator's own statement ----
#
# Not an installation's to make, and read from this environment only, never
# from the installation document below: whether this deployment may point the
# installation's model at an address inside its own network -- a model running
# on the same host, say. Off by default, and a provider that names a private
# host is refused without it (ADR 0003).
# GILBERT_AGENT_ALLOW_PRIVATE_PROVIDER=1

# ---- Everything else lives in Stalwart ----
#
# The installation's own configuration is one document, `installation.json`, in
# the Master account's own `gilbert` app folder -- written by the server on
# first boot and edited at **Administration → Installation** from then on. It
# is read once at boot after the handshake above, and a redeploy reads back
# exactly what the last edit wrote, because that is where it lives.
#
# Nothing in this file sets any of it. Where the environment and the document
# both carry a name, the document decides for a booted process -- except the
# container's own facts above, whose stated values win -- and the environment's
# copy is only what a process with no boot runs on: a test, a tool, a
# development server started without a sign-in.
#
# The document holds, by section:
#
#   server      where it listens (the container's own HOST and PORT override
#               it), whether a proxy is trusted and which peers, the
#               Secure-cookie posture, the session cookie's name, whether the
#               JMAP response is compressed
#   limits      the upstream timeout, the largest upload, the remote-image
#               proxy, the sign-in rate limit, and the per-session budget on
#               the data path
#   sessions    how long a session lives, and how long "remember me" extends it
#   push        the live-update mode and whether the raw push relay is used
#   upstreams   the domains served by another Stalwart: domain to base URL
#   agent       the agent's timers (poll, heartbeat, lease), its bounds (chain
#               hops, pages, tokens), whether it may think or read images, how
#               many drafts it may read in a month, its health port, and
#               whether it runs in the server's own process
#   branding    the name this installation shows
#   secret      the app secret, generated on first boot, which derives the
#               encryption keys for sessions -- stated here by publishing the
#               document, because a secret the container holds is a secret a
#               redeploy loses
#
# One value has to be known before Stalwart can be asked, and so is a fact about
# the image rather than a setting: the prefix the web bundle was **built** with.
# The bundle writes its own asset URLs when it is built, so a build that does
# not know the prefix produces a shell that cannot load itself under one. It is
# one fact in two places, and they are the same variable: the build argument
# bakes the prefix into the bundle, and `BASE_PATH` in the running container
# tells the server which one to serve -- an empty value is the domain root.
#
#   docker build --build-arg BASE_PATH=/webmail .
#   docker run -e BASE_PATH=/webmail ...
#
# It is not the installation's to decide and not in its document: a document
# that could move it could disagree with the bundle, and a page that cannot load
# itself says nothing about why. A production process that states no prefix
# refuses to serve rather than guessing.
#
# The installation policy (what a person's settings are seeded with, what is
# locked, what changes have already applied) is a separate document per
# account, published from **Administration → Installation policy**. The shipped
# `settings-policy.example.json` is what that editor takes.
